* START-HISTORY:
* 17 Sep 26 Windows port - RELEASE_1.1 55: asks K$GROUP.MEMBER instead of
*           running PowerShell through os.execute.  A pre-authenticated API
*           session cannot start a child process at all (0xC0000142, measured
*           b190), so every caller in such a session got @false and the
*           account gate read it as "not granted".  Same live SAM question, no
*           child, and "could not tell" is now distinct from "not a member".
*           The description's own "COST TO KNOW ABOUT" paragraph predicted
*           this change; it is done, in C, as win32group.c.
* 15 Aug 26 Windows port - a group given as a SID is asked for with -SID.
*           Get-LocalGroupMember -Group refuses one, so "is this user an
*           administrator?" used to answer false for everybody.
* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - ask Windows instead of reading /etc/group, which
*           does not exist on the MSYS2 runtime.  See the description.
* rev 0.9.0 Jan 25 mab with the change in ownership of group ACCOUNTS, we need this routine to return true if a user is looking up their own group account.
* 20240219 mab initial commit
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    x = !is_grp_member(user,group)
*
*    user - Windows user name to test for membership of a Windows local group
*
*    This function sets the STATUS() value to 0 if successful, 1 if the
*    specified group does not exist.
*
*    WHY THIS NO LONGER READS /etc/group.  It used to open /etc and read
*    "group" as a text file.  MSYS2 and Cygwin dropped /etc/passwd and
*    /etc/group in favour of direct SAM lookups, so the open failed, the
*    routine set status 1 and returned false for EVERYONE, and the sdusers
*    test at LOGIN terminated every connection with "This user is not
*    registered for SD use".  Nothing about it looked like a missing file.
*    See PROJECT_STATUS.md section 6.
*
*    K$GROUP.MEMBER is asked instead - a live NetLocalGroupGetMembers lookup
*    in C (gplsrc/win32group.c) - and it distinguishes the three answers the
*    caller needs, which parsing "net localgroup" output does not do without
*    depending on the language Windows is installed in:
*
*       1    a member                -> @true,  status 0
*       0    not a member            -> @false, status 0
*      -1    COULD NOT TELL          -> @false, status 1
*
*    Membership is compared on the last backslash-delimited component, so
*    MACHINE\don and don both match don.  A group may be named as a SID, which
*    win32group.c resolves before the lookup.
*
*    WHAT REPLACED THE POWERSHELL, AND WHY.  This ran Get-LocalGroupMember
*    through os.execute until 17 Sep 2026.  The paragraph that stood here
*    called that a cost worth knowing about - "slow enough to notice on a path
*    that runs at every login" - and said the fast answer was a KERNEL key but
*    "that is new C code and this is not".  RELEASE_1.1 55 forced the issue:
*    an API session spawned AS the user cannot start ANY child process, so the
*    PowerShell answer was not slow there, it was unobtainable.  The C is now
*    written.  getgrnam() was NOT the route taken - in_group() answers only
*    about the CALLING process's own groups and cannot be asked about another
*    user - so the key does a live lookup by name, which also keeps the
*    revocation semantics apisrvr's sdapi test depends on.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
function is_grp_member(user,group)
$catalog !is_grp_member

* 17 Sep 26 Windows port - this file had no $include at all; K$GROUP.MEMBER
*   needs one.  The header is $defines only.  (It arrived as a temporary
*   diagnostic aid and is now load-bearing - do not remove it with the
*   diagnostics.)
$include int$keys.h

deffun valid_os_name(name) calling '!valid_os_name'

user_found = @false
set.status 0

* Modified by Composer AI - 2026/06/10.
* Empty user or group cannot match membership; skip the lookup.
if user = '' or group = '' then
   set.status 1
   return user_found
end
* --------------------

* rev 0.9.0  is this the user's own group account?
if upcase(user) = upcase(group) then return @true

* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - both names are interpolated into a shell command
*   below, so they are validated first.  !valid_os_name permits letters,
*   digits, dot, underscore and hyphen only, which is what makes the quoting
*   below sufficient rather than merely tidy.
* 17 Sep 26 Windows port - ***THE SHELL COMMAND IS GONE AND THIS CHECK STAYS,
*   ON PURPOSE.***  There is no quoting left to protect, so the reason above no
*   longer applies - but the check is still what refuses a malformed name
*   cheaply and with status 1, and it is what keeps the @fm-joined argument to
*   K$GROUP.MEMBER free of anything that could be read as a separator.  Kept
*   with its reason corrected rather than deleted with its reason.
if not(valid_os_name(user)) or not(valid_os_name(group)) then
   set.status 1
   return user_found
end

* 15 Aug 26 Windows port - A SID NAMES A GROUP TOO, and it must keep working:
*   asking whether somebody is a Windows administrator HAS to be asked by SID,
*   because the name is translated on a localised Windows.  When this ran
*   PowerShell that meant -SID rather than -Group (measured 15 Aug 2026:
*   -Group "S-1-5-32-544" answered "not found", so the check silently answered
*   FALSE for every administrator alive).  win32group.c now resolves the SID
*   itself, so the requirement survives the mechanism that carried it.
* 17 Sep 26 Windows port - RELEASE_1.1 55.  ***NO CHILD PROCESS.***  This ran
*   PowerShell's Get-LocalGroupMember through os.execute, and a
*   PRE-AUTHENTICATED API session - one spawned AS the user by K$HANDOFF -
*   CANNOT START POWERSHELL AT ALL.  Measured on b190: CreateProcess succeeds
*   and the child dies in DLL initialisation with 0xC0000142, because the
*   session is on a non-interactive S4U token pointed at winsta0\default and
*   user32 cannot attach; sd.exe itself survives only because a POSIX console
*   program never loads user32.  The front, still LocalSystem, answered the
*   same question in the same run - so it was never the data.
*
*   K$GROUP.MEMBER IS THE SAME QUESTION, LIVE, IN C (win32group.c): a
*   NetLocalGroupGetMembers lookup by NAME.  Not K$IN.GROUP, whose in_group()
*   reads the CALLING process's own groups and cannot be asked about another
*   user.  Keeping a LIVE query rather than a token test also keeps the
*   semantics apisrvr's sdapi test relies on - a grant removed in Windows
*   takes effect on the next connection, with no sign-out.
*
*   ***THREE ANSWERS, AND THE THIRD IS WHY THIS WAS SILENT FOR SEVEN RUNS.***
*   -1 is "could not tell" - a lookup that failed, or a group that does not
*   exist - and it is NOT the same as 0.  It still returns @false, because
*   failing closed is right for an access check, but it sets STATUS 1 so a
*   caller can tell the two apart, and the reason is on the audit trail
*   (op_kernel.c writes it).  The old code could not make that distinction:
*   a shell that never ran and a real "no" both arrived as @false.
   grp.answer = kernel(K$GROUP.MEMBER, user : @fm : group)

   begin case
      case grp.answer = 1
         user_found = @true
      case grp.answer = 0
         user_found = @false
      case 1
         * Could not tell.  Fail closed, and say so in status().
         user_found = @false
         set.status 1
   end case

return user_found
end

* END-CODE
